  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "A fast-food chain wants to open some new restaurants. The company estimates that its annual profit, in hundreds of thousands of dollars, is given by\n\n$$\nP(x)=-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-12x+20,\n$$\n\nwhere $x$ is the number of new restaurants it opens up. If the company has a budget to open up between $1$ and $8$ restaurants, how many restaurants should the company open to maximize its profit?",
  "worked_solution": "Since $x$ represents the number of  new restaurants opened, and since the company can open between $1$ and $8$ restaurants, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 8.\n$$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<8.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=-{x}^{2}+8x-12.\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+8x-12 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-8x+12) & =0 \\\\ -(x-2)(x-6) & =0 \\\\ x & =2,6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to find which of $x=2$ or $x=6$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-2x+8\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=2$ and $x=6$ into ${P}^{″}(x),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{″}(2) & =-2(2)+8=4>0, \\\\ {P}^{″}(6) & =-2(6)+8=-4<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative at $x=6,$ the point $x=6$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=6$ and $x=8.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3}+4(1)^{2}-12(1)+20=\\frac{35}{3}≈12 \\\\ P(6) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(6)^{3}+4(6)^{2}-12(6)+20=20\\,✓ \\\\ P(8) & =-\\frac{1}{3}(8)^{3}+4(8)^{2}-12(8)+20=\\frac{28}{3}≈9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=6$ is the global maximum of $P(x).$ So, the company must open $6$ restaurants to maximize its profit.",
